Context you must understand before editing

Alva is a vanilla JavaScript browser fighting game.

The central application controller is js/core/app.js. It creates app.settings, ScreenManager, MenuNavigator, loading/confirmation overlays, registers all screens, starts at SplashScreen, and runs the animation loop.

The splash flow is in js/screens/splash-screen.js. At present it finishes by navigating to Home.

The Home UI is in js/screens/home-screen.js. Its menu currently contains Play, Watch Mode, Practice Ground, Discover, and Settings. Settings is currently a normal full screen reached through that fifth Home menu button.

The current Settings screen is js/screens/settings-screen.js. It only manages the mobile control scheme, with Joystick and Classic Buttons options.

Persistent user preferences are deliberately centralized in js/core/settings.js. It is currently the only game module that directly touches localStorage, using the alva.settings key and a versioned object. Preserve this architectural rule: no other module should directly read or write localStorage.

Touch controls live in js/game/touch-controls.js, with their positioning and sizes primarily defined in styles.css. There are two existing schemes: joystick and classic. Both must continue driving exactly the same internal input actions. Quick Battle and Practice Ground create TouchControls; Watch Mode intentionally has no player touch controls.

Overlay keyboard/gamepad handling already has a good pattern. js/ui/overlays.js uses app.nav.pushScope() / popScope(), and js/core/menu-navigator.js supports modal navigation, Back/Escape handling, spatial navigation, focus management, and gamepad controls. Reuse that pattern instead of building a second navigation system.

The game already has extensive dependency-free Node tests, especially tests/settings.test.mjs, tests/controls-ui.test.mjs, tests/splash-screen.test.mjs, tests/battle-screen.test.mjs, tests/practice-ground.test.mjs, and tests/watch-mode.test.mjs.

Goal

Add a proper player-preferences system with English/French language selection, replace the Home Settings menu item with a top-right settings icon that opens a translucent modal dialog, and add a full touch-control layout editor allowing players to freely move and resize their mobile controls.

The feature must feel native to the existing Alva UI rather than like a separate tool bolted onto it.

First-launch language selection

The first time a player launches Alva on a device, show a one-time language-selection dialog offering exactly:

English

Français

Do not force the player through the normal Settings UI first.

This first-run dialog should appear before the normal game experience begins, ideally before the splash sequence so that once a language is chosen all subsequent player-facing UI can use the selected language. A clean flow is:

App initialization → determine whether a valid language preference exists → if not, display the language dialog over the initial black background → player selects English or Français → persist the preference → start the normal splash → Home.

Returning players with a stored language preference must not see this first-run dialog again.

The first-run language chooser itself should be understandable without an existing locale. Use a neutral/bilingual presentation such as a title that clearly communicates language selection and buttons whose names are self-identifying: “English” and “Français”.

The language choice must be persisted through the existing Settings abstraction, never by directly using localStorage from the dialog.

If storage is unavailable or blocked, keep the selected language for the current session just as the existing settings implementation does. It is acceptable that it cannot survive a future browser visit when persistent storage itself is unavailable.

Do not mistake “default to English” for “the player selected English.” The application needs to know whether the initial language choice has actually been completed so the one-time prompt works correctly.

