Touch-control layout editor

This is a real editor, not a handful of fixed presets.

The player must be able to freely reposition every visible mobile gameplay control and freely change its size.

For Classic Buttons, the editable controls include Move Left, Charge/C, Move Right, and all six right-side action buttons.

For Joystick, the editable controls include the joystick itself, its Charge button, both mouvement/Dash buttons, and all six right-side action buttons.

Treat the joystick as an editable control even though it is not a <button>.

Every editable control must have a stable control ID that is independent of its translated label.

The editor should display a representative landscape game/control surface so players can position controls where they will actually appear.

Dragging a control moves it.

Selecting a control must provide an explicit size adjustment UI, such as a slider and/or +/- controls, so resizing is possible without requiring a multitouch pinch gesture. Pointer pinch resizing may be added but must not be the only way.

Changes should preview immediately.

Include a “Reset to defaults” action that restores the exact current Alva layout for the selected scheme.

Include a clear Done/Back action.

Save edits automatically or when leaving the editor, but the behavior must be deterministic and covered by tests.

Players should be able to customize Joystick and Classic layouts independently. Switching control scheme later must restore that scheme’s own saved layout.

Responsive layout representation

Do not persist raw desktop pixel coordinates.

Store positions in a resolution-independent form so a layout customized on one landscape viewport remains usable on another.

Use normalized coordinates or percentages relative to the touch-control viewport/safe play area. Store each control around its center, for example an x, y, and scale.

At runtime translate normalized positions into the current viewport.

Respect safe-area insets.

Clamp controls so their usable hit area cannot be moved completely off-screen.

Allow meaningful freedom, including moving controls away from their default clusters, but prevent permanently inaccessible configurations.

Choose sensible scale limits. They should provide clearly smaller and clearly larger controls without allowing zero-sized or absurdly giant buttons. Something in the approximate 0.6–1.8x range is reasonable unless the existing geometry suggests better limits.

A control’s visual size and touch hitbox must scale together.

Window resize/orientation changes must recompute positions correctly.

Default behavior when no custom layout exists must remain pixel-for-pixel or effectively visually equivalent to the current layout.

